Subject: [PATCH] drivers: i2c: stm32_v2: DEV-only interrupt forensics hooks

Baseline: zephyr v3.6.0 (468eb56cf242eedba62006ee758700ee6148763f)
Applies to: drivers/i2c/i2c_ll_stm32_v2.c (interrupt mode) and
drivers/i2c/Kconfig.stm32. Independent of 0001, which touches the
completion paths further down the same file.

WHY WE NEED IT, measured on DS20001 2026-09-23. The L7 diagnostic image
stopped every thread on the board in the same millisecond -- the
acquisition thread inside an i2c2 read, the system work queue and the CAN
poll loop, with no transfer of any kind in flight -- while the 1 s timer
callback that feeds the watchdog went on running for another 5.7 s. Thread
level dead, interrupt level alive. The recorded state cannot say why,
because everything that could have recorded it is a thread.

The suspicion is this driver's event ISR: it advances current.buf and
current.len whenever current.len != 0, without first checking that this
entry actually carried RXNE or TXIS. An entry carrying neither consumes a
byte that never arrived; the real byte then finds current.len == 0, is
never read out of RXDR, and RXNE stays asserted with RXIE enabled -- an
interrupt nothing will clear again. Every thread starves, including the one
whose 500 ms transfer timeout has already expired in kernel time.

That is a hypothesis about an interrupt, so it has to be measured in one.
This patch adds two hooks, called on entry to the event and error
interrupts before any flag is cleared and before current.* is advanced,
carrying what the interrupt saw. The application counts entries, buckets
them by flag, and keeps per-second rates in a RAM region that survives the
reset -- in particular "len != 0 with neither RXNE nor TXIS" (the phantom
byte) and "RXNE with len == 0" (the uncleanable interrupt).

The option defaults to n and no released image sets it, so production
builds are byte for byte unchanged; that is checked by the usual flag-off
A/B comparison. When it is on, the build requires the application to
provide both symbols, which is deliberate: a diagnostic image that silently
lost its instrumentation would be worse than one that fails to link.

Enabling it is not free, and the help text says so rather than claiming the
hooks are invisible. They take no lock, no log and no clock on their fast
path, but they do lengthen every I2C interrupt, and what they are hunting
is a race about interrupt timing: an instrumented run that stops
reproducing a hang has moved the window, not fixed anything.

This is instrumentation, not a fix. If the measurement confirms the race,
the fix is the upstream rework of this ISR (or the minimal equivalent), not
this patch.

---
--- a/drivers/i2c/i2c_ll_stm32_v2.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/i2c_ll_stm32_v2.c
@@ -27,6 +27,18 @@
 
 #include "i2c-priv.h"
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_I2C_STM32_LEXX_ISR_FORENSICS
+/* DEV ONLY. Implemented by the application (lexxpluss_apps/src/tof_diag_i2c.cpp), which is the only
+ * thing that turns this option on. The hooks are called on entry to each interrupt, before any flag
+ * is cleared and before current.buf/current.len are advanced, so what they record is what the ISR
+ * itself saw. They must not block, log or clear anything.
+ */
+void lexx_i2c_forensics_event(const struct device *dev, uint32_t isr, uint32_t cr1, uint32_t cr2,
+			      uint32_t sw_len, const uint8_t *sw_buf);
+void lexx_i2c_forensics_error(const struct device *dev, uint32_t isr, uint32_t cr1, uint32_t cr2,
+			      uint32_t sw_len, const uint8_t *sw_buf);
+#endif
+
 #define STM32_I2C_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT_MSEC  500
 
 static inline void msg_init(const struct device *dev, struct i2c_msg *msg,
@@ -363,6 +375,11 @@
 	struct i2c_stm32_data *data = dev->data;
 	I2C_TypeDef *i2c = cfg->i2c;
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_I2C_STM32_LEXX_ISR_FORENSICS
+	lexx_i2c_forensics_event(dev, i2c->ISR, i2c->CR1, i2c->CR2, data->current.len,
+				 data->current.buf);
+#endif
+
 #if defined(CONFIG_I2C_TARGET)
 	if (data->slave_attached && !data->master_active) {
 		stm32_i2c_slave_event(dev);
@@ -426,6 +443,11 @@
 	struct i2c_stm32_data *data = dev->data;
 	I2C_TypeDef *i2c = cfg->i2c;
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_I2C_STM32_LEXX_ISR_FORENSICS
+	lexx_i2c_forensics_error(dev, i2c->ISR, i2c->CR1, i2c->CR2, data->current.len,
+				 data->current.buf);
+#endif
+
 #if defined(CONFIG_I2C_TARGET)
 	if (data->slave_attached && !data->master_active) {
 		/* No need for a slave error function right now. */
--- a/drivers/i2c/Kconfig.stm32
+++ b/drivers/i2c/Kconfig.stm32
@@ -38,6 +38,29 @@
 	help
 	  Enable Interrupt support for the I2C Driver
 
+config I2C_STM32_LEXX_ISR_FORENSICS
+	bool "DEV: LexxPluss I2C interrupt forensics hooks"
+	depends on I2C_STM32_V2 && I2C_STM32_INTERRUPT
+	default n
+	help
+	  DEV ONLY, and off in every released image. Calls two application
+	  supplied hooks on entry to the I2C event and error interrupts,
+	  passing the ISR/CR1/CR2 the interrupt saw together with the
+	  driver's current.len and current.buf. It exists to measure whether
+	  a wedged bus is an interrupt that can no longer be cleared, which
+	  no thread level instrumentation can observe because such a bus
+	  starves every thread.
+
+	  The hooks only count and store, and take no lock, no log and no
+	  clock on their fast path -- but they do lengthen every I2C
+	  interrupt, and the fault they are looking for is a race about
+	  interrupt timing. Enabling this can therefore move the window it
+	  is meant to catch; if an instrumented run stops reproducing a
+	  hang, that is not evidence the hang is gone. It also makes the
+	  build depend on the application providing both symbols, which is
+	  deliberate: a diagnostic image that silently lost its
+	  instrumentation would be worse than one that fails to link.
+
 config I2C_STM32_COMBINED_INTERRUPT
 	bool
 	depends on I2C_STM32_INTERRUPT
